+## Deviations from the spec (with reasons)
+
+1. **Pool size 8, not 3 (spec §1.2 says N=3).** `graphics/zerocopy.go` documents — with a live
+   marquee-jitter regression as the evidence — that the encoder holds 5–6 buffers in flight (queue +
+   appsink + nvenc), so a 3-slot pool starves and dups most frames. I mirrored the proven GL pool size
+   (8); each slot is only w·h·4 of VRAM. A literal N=3 would reproduce a known starvation bug.
+2. **`glTexStorageMem2DEXT` (target-bound) instead of `glTextureStorageMem2DEXT` (DSA).** Same
+   `EXT_memory_object` entry-point family; the bound variant avoids the DSA
+   texture-target-initialisation pitfall (a `glGenTextures` name never bound can `INVALID_OPERATION` on
+   `glTextureStorage*`). Storage semantics identical.
+3. **GL EXT functions resolved directly in C** (opengl32 `GetProcAddress` + `wglGetProcAddress`, the
+   RGFW sentinel guard) rather than threading a getProcAddress from Go — self-contained, no GL header
+   dependency in the translation unit, and the WGL context is current on the calling cothread.
+4. **Automatic missing-extension fallback is realised as a build-time capability probe.** Spec §1.1
+   wants zero-copy to fall back to readback automatically when an extension is missing, but the
+   readback-vs-GL pipeline is a BUILD-time property (chosen before the core's device exists). A
+   throwaway-instance probe (no device) makes that decision deterministic and knowable up front; the
+   in-`create_device_wrapper` check is the belt-and-braces runtime guard. On the fleet's NVIDIA hardware
+   both extensions are universally present, so the probe passes and the deterministic fallbacks are the
+   kill-switch and `CLOUD_GAME_VK_ZC_SIMULATE_NOEXT`.
+5. **Geometry-flip realloc keeps a retired generation one flip** (defer-by-one) rather than blocking
+   until GStreamer releases every slot. Blocking on every 448↔896 flip (which the M1 results show
+   fires repeatedly on the menu cycle) would stall the cothread; defer-by-one protects the shared memory
+   from a use-after-free while flips are ≥ the encoder depth apart. See open question 2.
+
+---
+
+## Open questions for review
+
+1. **Sync layer choice is unresolved without the live motion test.** Layer-1 (semaphore) is the
+   default and the spec's preferred mechanism; layer-2 (fence) is wired as the A/B reference. Which
+   ships depends on step 2/5 of the runbook. I did not guess.
+2. **Rapid geometry flips vs defer-by-one.** If PS2 ever flips extent faster than the encoder's ~6-frame
+   depth for several consecutive flips, a retired generation could be destroyed while GStreamer still
+   holds one of its textures. Defer-by-one covers the observed menu-cycle rate; a pathological
+   every-few-frames flip would want defer-by-N or an all-slots-released gate. Worth confirming under the
+   live flip test (runbook step 3).
+3. **No GL→Vk "done" semaphore.** Slot reuse is gated purely by GStreamer's release notify (the slot is
+   only re-acquired after the encoder consumed it), mirroring the GL path which has no reuse semaphore
+   either. If the live motion test shows reuse corruption, a GL→Vk done semaphore per slot is the fix.
+4. **`skip_hw_context_destroy` interaction.** The M1 finding (ps2-Vulkan must ship with the hack, the
+   MTGS::CloseGS deadlock is renderer-independent) carries into M2 unchanged; the zc teardown runs
+   before the (skipped) context_destroy, so the order is unaffected. Unvalidated live, like the rest.
+
