+**How the crash was cracked (method, for the file).** The GPF PC *varied across runs* (0x1407498c0,
+0x1407499b0, 0x0, **0x1**) — the signature of a **call through a garbage function pointer**, not a fixed
+null-deref. The GIPA-instrumentation hypothesis was exonerated (746 requests, 44 NULLs, all benign KHR-
+aliases/optional-EXT). What named the culprit: a **RelWithDebInfo flycast build** whose GPF handler was
+patched to **stack-walk `[rsp]`** and print each return address's module+RVA. RET[0] resolved to
+**worker.exe RVA 0x7499b3 = our own `run_loop` (`nanoarch.c:388` `((void(*)(void))call.fn)()`)** dispatching
+`Nan0.Video.hw.context_reset` — i.e. the dangling pointer. The earlier "flycast DLL" PCs were also
+worker.exe (base 0x140000000), originally misattributed.
+
+**GPF stack-walk diagnostic — where it lives, how to resurrect it (NOT shipped in the release patch).**
+Deliberately kept OUT of `flycast-custom-core.patch` so the production release core stays minimal. It is a
+local edit to `D:\Arcade\build\flycast\core\windows\fault_handler.cpp` (in the `[GPF]` handler at ~line 156):
+after the existing PC print, a lambda `printModRva(tag, addr)` does `GetModuleHandleExA(FROM_ADDRESS|
+UNCHANGED_REFCOUNT)` + `GetModuleFileNameA` and prints `module=…base=…RVA=(addr-base)`; it prints the PC and
+then walks 12 stack slots from `context.rsp` (`RET[0..11]`) — the pushed return address of a garbage-target
+`call` names the caller. To resurrect for the next flycast crash: re-apply that edit, then build the
+**symbolized DLL**:
+```
+export PATH=/d/msys64/ucrt64/bin:$PATH
+cd /d/Arcade/build/flycast
+cmake -S . -B bld-dbg -G Ninja -DLIBRETRO=ON -DUSE_LIBCDIO=ON -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo
+cmake --build bld-dbg --target flycast_libretro --parallel 14
+cp bld-dbg/flycast_libretro.dll flycast_dbg_libretro.dll   # ~198 MB, DWARF-symboled
+```
+Deploy it over `…/assets/cores/flycast_custom_libretro.dll` (back up the release DLL first), reproduce, then
+`addr2line -f -C -e <the crashing module> <PC or RET RVA>`. The DLL loads at an ASLR base, so the printed
+`RVA` (not the raw PC) is what addr2line resolves. (`bld-dbg` + `flycast_dbg_libretro.dll` are already built.)
